<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Odoacer</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 433–493 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Odoacer was a Germanic military leader who deposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romulus-augustulus">Romulus Augustulus</a>, the last Western Roman emperor, on 4 September 476 CE. He sent the imperial regalia to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> and ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> as king. The event is conventionally taken as the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-roman-empire">Western Roman Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/odoacer.jpg" alt="Odoacer" width="320" height="348" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: British Museum (photo) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coin_of_Odoacer_at_the_British_Museum_(obverse).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Odoacer's origins are uncertain. A fragment of the historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/priscus">Priscus</a> describes him as a man of the Sciri, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edeco">Edeco</a> and brother of Hunulf. John of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> reported that he was 60 when he died in 493, which gives a birth around 433. Sources call him a Scirian, a Rugian, a Goth or a Thuringian, and many historians accept Scirian heritage; none of the ancient sources calls him a Hun. By 470 he was an officer in what remained of the Roman army.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 475 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orestes-roman-general">Orestes</a>, appointed commander by the emperor Julius Nepos, rebelled, drove Nepos from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and proclaimed his young son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romulus-augustulus">Romulus Augustulus</a> emperor. The Germanic foederati, who had been quartered in Italy for years, asked Orestes for lands of their own, and when he refused they turned to Odoacer. Orestes was killed at Placentia, and the foederati proclaimed Odoacer rex on 23 August 476. He advanced to Ravenna and compelled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romulus">Romulus</a> to abdicate on 4 September, sparing him with a pension of 6,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solidus">solidi</a>, according to the Anonymus Valesianus.</p><p>The Senate then sent an embassy to the Eastern emperor Zeno, saying that one monarch sufficed to rule the world, and Zeno granted Odoacer the title of patrician and authority to govern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> as dux Italiae. Odoacer ruled with the cooperation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-senate">Roman Senate</a>, nominating senators to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/consul">consulship</a> and other high offices. Although an Arian Christian, he had good relations with the church hierarchy. After Nepos was murdered on 9 May 480, he executed the assassins and established his rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Zeno came to see Odoacer as a rival, and promised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodoric-the-great">Theodoric the Great</a> and his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostrogoths">Ostrogoths</a> the Italian peninsula if they removed him. Theodoric defeated Odoacer at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soca">Isonzo</a>, at Verona and on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adda">Adda</a>, and besieged him in Ravenna. A treaty negotiated by John, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Ravenna, on 25 February 493 provided that the two would share Ravenna and rule jointly. Theodoric entered the city on 5 March and killed Odoacer with his own sword at a banquet on 15 March, and then had Odoacer's followers hunted down.</p><p>Odoacer's wife Sunigilda was stoned to death, his brother Onoulphus was killed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> in a church, and his son Thela, exiled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>, was killed after trying to return. The historian J. B. Bury saw Odoacer's seizure of power as one stage in the dismemberment of the Empire rather than its fall.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Odoacer” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When <b>Odoacer</b> deposed the last Western emperor in 476, he simply sent the imperial regalia to Constantinople, and contemporaries barely remarked on it.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-empire">Roman Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>By the time <b>Odoacer</b> deposed the boy emperor Romulus Augustulus in 476, the western state had been shrinking for a generation into something closer to an Italian kingdom with an imperial title.</blockquote><a href="/essays/the-collapse-problem">The Collapse Problem</a></li><li><blockquote>After Orestes refused the Germanic foederati land in Italy, they and much of the Italic Roman army proclaimed <b>Odoacer</b> king.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-23">23 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Sent by Emperor Zeno to overthrow <b>Odoacer</b>, king of Italy, Theodoric won a battle on the banks of the Isontius River (now the Isonzo), not far from Aquileia, soon after arriving in Italy.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-28">28 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>After a long siege ended in a power-sharing agreement, Theoderic murdered <b>Odoacer</b> at a banquet.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-15">15 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Less than a year later <b>Odoacer</b> deposed him, an event conventionally taken as the end of the Western Roman Empire.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-31">31 October in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Pannonia Prima. Died in Ravenna, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.42,12.2&amp;year=472" aria-label="Ravenna on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4842 593 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Odoacer"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5292 863h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5292 863h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=44.42,12.2&amp;year=472">What was here in 472 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
